 [17592186089978 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8c5e7f77-3f25-5126-8f63-eef907893f01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089979 #edb/ref 1080 "18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089979 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089979 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e32ed80f-6660-5c9a-82ae-0dcc0a4e0f00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089980 #edb/ref 1080 "24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089980 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089980 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59126649-2842-565d-9ad6-f6cb17b72dfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089981 #edb/ref 1080 "36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089981 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089981 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "660384a1-823d-58de-ada5-b7ce9db11f79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089982 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089982 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089982 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089982 #edb/ref 1077 "field-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089982 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ca561fb4-c5ec-5da1-96d4-198edd64ea7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089983 #edb/ref 1080 "less than" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089983 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089983 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3fb07b2-242b-5505-8017-d095b7160741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089984 #edb/ref 1080 "greater than" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089984 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089984 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65788e60-7fd3-5a45-8c92-f7efaf59b5e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091943 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091943 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n> $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template outlined below?\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $000000$\n> $0000$\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $000000$ We have\n> $000000$\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $000000$\n> **Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "493f9465-dfd4-5103-8aba-e02d92282172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091943 #edb/ref 1074 "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n > 2n\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case**. Since we are required to prove the statement for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3\n$$\n\nthe base case is $P(3)$. So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=3}$.\n> $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^3} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(3)}$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show that the inequality holds.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some\n\n$$\nn = k \\geq 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n)$.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k \\geq 3}$, we have\n> $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^k} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2k}$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. We start by writing down $P(k+1)$.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2^{k+1}} > \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2(k+1)}$.\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$, the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n> **Conclusion**: Therefore, $2^n > 2n$ for all $n \\geq 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091949 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091949 #edb/ref 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3, n\\in \\mathbb N$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b8b1014-56bf-51c0-be9e-a194181a0d99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091949 #edb/ref 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots$, where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nn^2 > 2n+2 \\quad\\text{for}\\quad n \\geq 3\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3\n$$\n\nthe base case is $P(3)$. So, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\n> We proceed using induction on $n$.\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $3^{2} = 9$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $2(3) + 2 = 8$,\n> Thus, our statement is true for $n=3$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k\\geq 3\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 3$, we have\n> $k^2 > 2k+2$.\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $(k+1)^2 > 2(k+1)+2. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\n> We write\n> $(k + 1)^{2}|= k^{2} + 2k + 1$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $k^{2} + 2k + 1|= (k^{2})_(⏟)_(> 2k + 2) + 2k + 1; > 2k + 2 + 2k + 1; = 2k + 2 + 2k_(⏟)_(> 1) + 1; > 2(k + 1) + 2$,\n> as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2 > 2n+2$ for all $n \\geq 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091950 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091950 #edb/ref 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for $n\\in\\mathbb N$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5d8d9e3-d45c-5ad9-aae7-7933e6f4fade" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091950 #edb/ref 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3), \\ldots$, where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n- $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb N$.\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n2^n +1 \\leq 3^n \\quad\\text{for} \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1)$. To do this, we substitute $n = 1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\n> We proceed using induction on $n$.\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n = 1$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $2^{1} + 1 = 3$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $3^{1} = 3$,\n> Thus, our statement is true for $n = 1$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn = k \\geq 1\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1$, we have\n> $2^k +1 \\leq 3^k$.\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $2^{k+1} + 1\\leq 3^{k+1}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\n> We write\n> $2^{k + 1} + 1|= 2^{k} \\cdot 2^{1} + 1; = 2(2^{k}) + 1; = 2^{k} + 2^{k} + 1$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $2^{k} + 2^{k} + 1_(⏟)_(\\le 3^{k}) \\mid \\le 2^{k} + 3^{k}; = 2^{k}_(⏟)_(< 3^{k} < 2 \\cdot 3^{k}) + 3^{k}; < 2 \\cdot 3^{k} + 3^{k}; = 3 \\cdot 3^{k}; = 3^{k + 1}$,\n> as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is less than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction, $2^n +1 \\leq 3^n$ for all $n \\geq 1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092189 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092189 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092189 #edb/ref 1039 "Completing a Proof Template" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092189 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092189 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e61d3403-80d9-5633-b579-dcf452dbc265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092190 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092190 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092190 #edb/ref 1039 "Proving Polynomial Inequalities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092190 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092190 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b83c640f-c7fd-5adc-94b8-b129d357b140" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092191 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092191 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092191 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092191 #edb/ref 1039 "Proving Other Inequalities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092191 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092191 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "82830730-a9bc-57bd-9114-d0d50473ea63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1045 "Proving Inequalities Using Induction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092428 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4afd7cce-79fd-54bd-b3c9-a093406d5b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1071 "Use mathematical induction to prove that $6(n + 1) < n^{3}$ for $n \\ge 3, n ∈ N$.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n$.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n$6^{2}$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n = 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e55b1bb6-4fdf-5aaa-8b3c-9ba7f8dbcdd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093002 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093002 #edb/ref 1061 182504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093002 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e905cb3-e114-5070-9e69-88067966fecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093002 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093003 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093003 #edb/ref 1061 182505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6c5f326-acbf-56e7-a8fa-2ee4f6d2ce6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093004 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093004 #edb/ref 1061 182506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093004 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8c2174f-bc0e-5501-bccc-c8d28d3a0a3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093004 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093005 #edb/ref 1061 182507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ef74ebf-152a-5ae1-9441-667c8f0dda9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093006 #edb/ref 1061 182510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a2f8cf6-aca3-5e93-8f24-9846e22e5d40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093007 #edb/ref 1061 182513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a66ff57-4f1e-549c-9e1e-95f385befca5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093494 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the **principle of induction** states the following:\n\n> *Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), \\, P(2), \\, P(3), \\, \\ldots$, where*\n> - *$P(1)$ is true, and*\n> - *$P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N$.*\n> *Then, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N$.*\n\nLet's recall our general strategy for proving results using induction:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how induction is used to prove inequality statements. But first, let's discuss an important property of numbers often used in these proofs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093494 #edb/ref 1065 7933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093494 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c585281c-7bd0-57d6-b354-43229b6e8cf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093494 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093497 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's prove that the following statement is true using mathematical induction:\n\n> $n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n> $P(n): \\quad n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nthe base case is $P(1)$. So, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\n> **Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1$.\n> For the left-hand side, we have\n> $1^{2} + 3(1) = 4$,\n> and for the right-hand side, we have\n> $2(1) - 1 = 1$,\n> Thus, since $4 > 1$, our statement is true for $n=1$.\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some\n\n$$\nn=k\\geq 1\n$$\n\n> **Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1$, we have\n> $k^2+3k > 2k-1$.\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$. Let's start by writing down $P(k+1)$. This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n> **Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n> $(k+1)^2+ 3(k+1) > 2(k+1)-1 = 2k+1 \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\n> We write\n> $(k + 1)^{2} + 3(k + 1)|= k^{2} + 2k + 1 + 3k + 3; = k^{2} + 3k + 2k + 4$.\n> By the inductive hypothesis, we have\n> $k^{2} + 3k + 2k + 4|= (k^{2} + 3k)_(⏟)_(> 2k - 1) + 2k + 4; > 2k - 1 + 2k + 4; = (2k + 2) + (2k + 1); = 2k + 2_(⏟)_(> 0) + 2k + 1; > 2k + 1$,\n> as required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb N$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093497 #edb/ref 1065 9168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093497 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "34de351e-9875-51d8-954e-c36039a677e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093497 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093498 #edb/ref 1064 "When proving inequalities using induction, we often use a property of integers known as **transitivity of inequality.**\n\nIn particular, if $a, b$, and $c$ are integers, then the transitivity of inequality states the following:\n\n- If $a > b$ and $b > c$, then $a > c$\n- If $a \\geq b$ and $b \\geq c$, then $a \\geq c$\n- If $a < b$ and $b < c$, then $a < c$\n- If $a \\leq b$ and $b \\leq c$, then $a \\leq c$\n\nFor example:\n\n- Since $3 > 2$ and $2 > 1$, transitivity of inequality implies that $3 > 1$.\n- Since $2 \\leq 3$ and $3 \\leq 5$, then transitivity of inequality implies that $2 \\leq 5$.\n\nLet's now see how induction can be used to prove an inequality statement." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093498 #edb/ref 1065 9170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093498 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9295857c-f317-5f8b-a656-9bcf405921e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093498 #edb/ref 1067 "Transitivity of Inequality" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
